<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahikeng</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mafikeng, Mafeking</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahikeng, formerly Mafikeng or Mafeking, is the capital of North West province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It grew from a settlement of the Tshidi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a> in the mid-19th century and was the administrative headquarters of the Bechuanaland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>, though outside it, until 1965. It is famous for the siege of 1899–1900 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, defended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-baden-powell-1st-baron-baden-powell">Robert Baden-Powell</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahikeng.jpg" alt="Mahikeng" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: South African Tourism from South Africa · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mafikeng,_North_West,_South_Africa_(20506154516).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mahikeng, in the classic Setswana language, means place of rocks, or place among rocks, and refers to volcanic rocks that gave temporary shelter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a> people hunting animals that came to drink at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molopo-river">Molopo River</a>. The town lies close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, built on open veld at about 1,500 metres by the banks of the Upper Molopo, 1,400 km northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> and 260 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>. It was long written Mafeking by British settlers, a spelling still used for the siege of 1899–1900, and it later became Mafikeng, the form in common speech.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town began as Molema's town. In 1857 Molema Tawana led an advance guard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a> to scout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molopo-river">Molopo River</a> and settled there, while the main body under his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montshiwa">Montshiwa</a> followed. Montshiwa felt unsafe because of the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and moved to Moshaneng, but Molema stayed and used diplomacy to keep the Boers from settling near the town. Montshiwa returned in 1876. Molema was a firm believer in Western education and opened a school for the Barolong; he died in 1882.</p><p>In 1882 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> set up the Republic of Stellaland and the State of Goshen in Bechuanaland. Tswana chiefs sought British protection, and a force of 4,000 troops under Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-warren">Charles Warren</a> dissolved the two states in a campaign of 1884–1885. Bechuanaland was proclaimed a British protectorate with Mafeking as its capital, though the protectorate was later divided and Mafeking lay south of its boundary, in British Bechuanaland, later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. It remained the protectorate's administrative capital until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaborone">Gaborone</a> was established in 1965.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> broke out in 1899 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> besieged the town. The siege lasted 217 days, from October 1899 to May 1900, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-baden-powell-1st-baron-baden-powell">Robert Baden-Powell</a> a national hero; the use of adolescent boys as military auxiliaries helped lay the groundwork for the Scouting movement. British losses were 212 killed, soldiers and civilians, and more than 600 wounded, and Boer losses were significantly higher. In 1900 the British also built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> there for Boer women and children. A local referendum took the town into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bophuthatswana">Bophuthatswana</a> in 1980.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the end of apartheid in 1994 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bophuthatswana">Bophuthatswana</a> was reincorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and the merged Mafikeng and Mmabatho became the capital of the new North-West Province. The provincial legislature stands in the New Parliament Building in Mmabatho. The North-West University has a campus there, and the town has a museum, a railway station and the Mafikeng Game Reserve on its edge.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mafikeng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second Boer War of 1899–1902 Boer commandos invaded the Cape, besieging Kimberley and <b>Mafeking</b>, and several thousand Cape Afrikaners joined them as rebels.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When war with Britain came in 1899, Transvaal commandos besieged <b>Mafeking</b> and, with Free State forces, Ladysmith and Kimberley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tshidi under Montshiwa settled at <b>Mafikeng</b> and resisted Boer encroachment.</blockquote><a href="/state/barolong-chiefdom">Barolong Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Administration was minimal and ran from <b>Mafeking</b>, outside the territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/bechuanaland-protectorate">Bechuanaland Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>The colony secured the route along which the railway to <b>Mafeking</b> was later built.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-bechuanaland">British Bechuanaland</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1882 white volunteers who had fought for the Barolong chief Moshette against Montshiwa were rewarded with farms, and they proclaimed the Land Goshen with its headquarters at Rooigrond, near <b>Mafikeng</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/goshen">Goshen</a></li></ul></section>
